-- Measured real hits for the balance simulator (tools/balance/SimulateBalance.luau), at
-- FieldConfig.HitTimeScale 1.6, measured in Studio on a fresh save with the Studio-only
-- StudioLaunchSpeed attribute (38-degree loft, normal gravity). Re-measure after any change to
-- hit physics, the runway or HitTimeScale.
--
-- Distance is erratic in launch speed at every speed: the bounces make nearby speeds land far
-- apart (a sawtooth: each run of speeds starts long and falls off, then jumps up again), and
-- below about 200 studs/s the same speed also varies from hit to hit (up to ~40%). Above that a
-- speed usually repeats within ~3% in one session, but not always (336: 1,324 in one playtest,
-- 1,482 and 1,502 in the next).
--
-- points: the trend: the average of every hit within 5% of each speed (hits: how many), never
-- falling with speed; plus 55 and 70 (single hits, 2026-10-29) and 505 (3,906 / 3,928, the only
-- speed below 505 measured to repeat across sessions). The simulator interpolates between them
-- for speeds it has no hits for.
--
-- sweep: every hit below 505 (speeds 80-504 every 2, re-hit where ProgressionConfig uses them,
-- and the speeds the tables used before), {distance, seconds from swing acceptance to the
-- result}; seconds of a repeat hit are estimated from a similar hit. ProgressionConfig's
-- LaunchSpeedSteps for Levels 22-139 are speeds from here (SimulateBalance `measuredSteps`), and
-- the simulator looks them up exactly (their average).
--
-- erratic: from about 505 (measured 2026-10-29) the same story at higher speeds
-- (640 -> 4,310, 641 -> 3,395, 649 -> 7,000, 650 -> 5,959), each speed's trials {distance,
-- seconds}. They were measured in World 3 (the longest runway) unless noted; the same speed lands
-- alike in every World (660: 4,051 in World 3 and 4,045 in World 2; 687: 5,834 and 5,783). 7,000
-- means the ball reached World 3's end. Speeds 690 and 716 also reached World 1's end (twice /
-- once) and 665 and 716 World 2's. The speed table (ProgressionConfig.LaunchSpeedSteps from Level
-- 141) uses only speeds measured at least twice.
